 Theorem~\ref{thm:A} settles a companion closure question in the negative.  Every group is a quotient of a free group, and free groups are residually finite---hence LEF, hence sofic; presenting the nonsofic group of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} as such a quotient exhibits a residually finite group with a nonsofic quotient.  Consequently none of the three classes---residually finite, LEF, sofic---is closed under quotients.  This is also why the covers of Section~\ref{sec:fp} are proved nonsofic directly, from the forbidden table: a quotient-closure argument would have sufficed there, and none is valid.
 \end{remark}
 
+\begin{remark}[Two questions from Pestov's survey]\label{rem:pestov}%
+\leanpartial{\leanmod{Endpoint/MainResults}{exists_finitelyPresented_nonsofic_group,countable_nonsofic_groups_exist}\leanmod{Sofic/NearAction}{FullFinitelyAdditiveProbability,MeasurePreservingNearAction,EssentiallyFreeNearAction,AdmitsEssentiallyFreeNearAction,admitsEssentiallyFreeNearAction_of_isSofic,admitsEssentiallyFreeNearAction_trivial}}{Formalized: the finitely presented witness, and the sofic-to-near-action direction of the Elek--Szab\'o characterization, with closed witnesses.  Not formalized: the converse direction, quoted from the literature, which is what the negative answer to Question~5.3 consumes.}%
+Two further questions of Pestov's survey \cite{Pestov08} are settled by what is already proved.  Open Question~4.11 there asks whether every finitely presented group is hyperlinear (sofic).  Theorem~\ref{thm:C} answers the sofic half in the negative; the hyperlinear half remains open.  The finite obstruction behind the presentation is provided existentially, by Lemma~\ref{lem:models}; the argument does not compute it.
+
+Open Question~5.3 of the same survey asks whether every countable group admits an essentially free near action on a set equipped with a finitely additive probability measure defined on all of its subsets.  By the Elek--Szab\'o characterization \cite{ElekSzabo05}, stated as Theorem~5.2 of \cite{Pestov08}, a group admits such a near action exactly when it is sofic; the countable nonsofic group of Theorem~\ref{thm:A} therefore answers the question in the negative.  The formalized half of the equivalence is the constructive one: a sofic approximation, read along a nonprincipal ultrafilter, yields a finitely additive measure on the disjoint union of its models---the ultralimit of the normalized counting measures---and an essentially free near action there.  The direction the negative answer consumes is the quoted converse.  The statement rules out essentially free near actions carrying finitely additive measures on every subset; it asserts nothing about ordinary free probability-measure-preserving actions on standard probability spaces.
+\end{remark}
+
 \begin{remark}[Soficity is undecidable]\label{rem:undecidable}%
 \leanverified{\leanmod{Sofic/SoficTransfer}{isSofic_of_injective,SoficApproximation.restrict}\leanmod{Endpoint/MainResults}{exists_finitelyPresented_nonsofic_group}\leanmod{Sofic/SoficPositiveControl}{isSofic_of_finite}\leannote{Markov's two conditions are formalized; Adian--Rabin and Hall's embedding theorem are cited.}}%
 Soficity passes to subgroups, immediately from Definition~\ref{def:sofic}: a model for the ambient group, read along an injective homomorphism, is a model for the source---multiplicativity transports along the homomorphism, separation along the injection.  Every group into which a nonsofic group embeds is therefore nonsofic.
@@ -2418,6 +2425,8 @@ Fournier-Facio \cite{FournierFacio26} applies the criterion of Theorem~\ref{thm:
 Kun and Thom \cite{KunThom26} carry the mechanism of Section~\ref{sec:criterion} into ergodic theory and operator algebras.  They call \(\Gamma\leq G\) \emph{infranormal} when \(\set{g\in G\suchthat g\Gamma g^{-1}\leq\Gamma}\) generates \(G\)---the compression hypothesis \eqref{eq:gen} read as a condition on a subgroup---and prove that for an infranormal, non-normal Kazhdan pair the generalized wreath product \(\bigl(\bigoplus_{G/\Gamma}\Z/2\Z\bigr)\rtimes G\) is not sofic; that in a sofic Kazhdan group the centralizer of an infranormal Kazhdan subgroup is normal; and that the \(\Gamma\)-fixed algebra of a sofic probability-measure-preserving action is \(G\)-invariant, so that generalized Bernoulli actions over \(G/\Gamma\) are not sofic---which answers a question of P\u aunescu in the negative and yields nonsofic ergodic equivalence relations generated by free actions of residually finite groups.  They also exhibit explicit residually finite Kazhdan pairs \(\EL_r(\F_q[x_1,\dots,x_d])<\EL_r(\F_q[x_1^{\pm1},\dots,x_d^{\pm1}])\rtimes\operatorname{SL}_d(\Z)\) to which all of this applies.
 
 Alekseev and Thom \cite{AlekseevThom26}, in work independent of this paper and completed before it, reach the same phenomenon from the other side.  They show that the centralizer of a sofic embedding of a Kazhdan group into a metric ultraproduct of symmetric groups is again a metric ultraproduct of finite permutation groups, and deduce that when that centralizer acts ergodically on the Loeb space the group is LEF, hence residually finite if finitely presented.  Where Theorem~\ref{thm:D} uses compression to force a commuting subgroup to be LEF, they use ergodicity of the whole centralizer; both are readings of Kun's decomposition to the effect that a Kazhdan group cannot carry a sofic approximation whose centralizer is large.  Theorem~\ref{thm:A} is the extreme case, in which no sofic approximation exists at all.
+
+An earlier paper of the same authors supplies a conditional consequence that the present work discharges.  Alekseev and Thom prove \cite[Corollary~E]{AlekseevThom25} that if a nonsofic group exists, then some hyperbolic group admits a nonsofic probability-measure-preserving action: the Belegradek--Osin variation of the Rips construction embeds a finitely presented witness into an extension of a hyperbolic group, and a quasi-regular Bernoulli action carries the obstruction.  Theorems~\ref{thm:A} and~\ref{thm:C} discharge the hypothesis, so \emph{there is a hyperbolic group admitting a nonsofic p.m.p.\ action}; the conditional implication is entirely theirs, and this paper supplies the previously missing nonsofic group.  That corollary is independent of the Kun--Thom results above, which give nonsofic actions of residually finite---hence sofic---groups; neither statement subsumes the other.
 \end{remark}
 
